---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";

const title = "About VT Analyzer — Why We Built a YouTube Thumbnail Analyzer";
const description =
  "VT Analyzer helps creators optimize YouTube thumbnails with a free thumbnail checker built from real click-through-rate research, not generic design advice.";
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} path="/about/">
  <section class="bg-void py-16 text-mist sm:py-20">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 text-center">
      <p class="font-mono-num text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.25em] text-red">About</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 font-display text-4xl font-bold sm:text-5xl">Built by Creators Tired of Guessing</h1>
      <p class="mx-auto mt-4 max-w-xl text-mist/60">
        VT Analyzer exists because great videos still get skipped over when the thumbnail doesn't earn the click.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-16 px-5 py-20">
    <div>
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold text-ink dark:text-fog">Our Story</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink/65 dark:text-smoke sm:text-base">
        VT Analyzer started as an internal tool for our own channels. We kept noticing the same
        pattern: hours spent editing a video, and then a thumbnail thrown together in ten minutes
        that quietly capped how many people ever saw the work. Once we started treating the
        thumbnail as seriously as the video itself — testing contrast, text size, and composition
        before publishing — click-through rates moved in a way editing alone never did. What began
        as a spreadsheet of notes turned into the analyzer you're using today.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold text-ink dark:text-fog">Our Approach</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink/65 dark:text-smoke sm:text-base">
        A good thumbnail sits at the intersection of design and attention: it has to read instantly,
        at a tiny size, in a feed full of competing images. Generic design advice doesn't account
        for that context. Our scoring model is built specifically around how thumbnails are actually
        displayed on YouTube — mobile search results, the desktop homepage grid, and suggested
        videos — rather than treating a thumbnail like any other graphic.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-8 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="rounded-sm border border-ink/10 dark:border-seam bg-paper-dim dark:bg-night-dim p-6">
        <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-bold text-ink dark:text-fog">Our Mission</h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink/60 dark:text-smoke">
          Make professional-grade thumbnail feedback available to every creator, regardless of
          channel size or budget, so click-through rate stops being a guessing game.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-sm border border-ink/10 dark:border-seam bg-paper-dim dark:bg-night-dim p-6">
        <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-bold text-ink dark:text-fog">Our Vision</h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink/60 dark:text-smoke">
          A world where designing a thumbnail that both represents your content honestly and earns
          the click is a fast, confident step — not the last-minute afterthought it often is today.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="rounded-sm border border-red/20 bg-red/5 p-6">
      <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-bold text-ink dark:text-fog">Why Trust Our Analysis?</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink/60 dark:text-smoke">
        Our scoring weights are grounded in well-documented attention and readability
        principles — contrast, focal point, and legibility at small sizes — applied specifically
        to YouTube's display contexts. We're upfront that no tool can guarantee performance, since
        YouTube's algorithm, your content, and audience behavior all play a role too. See our
        <a href="/disclaimer/" class="text-red underline underline-offset-2">disclaimer</a> for the full picture.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold text-ink dark:text-fog">How the Analyzer Is Built</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink/65 dark:text-smoke sm:text-base">
        Rather than sending your image to a server, VT Analyzer processes every thumbnail directly
        inside your browser using the HTML5 Canvas API. When you upload or paste an image, the
        analyzer samples its pixels to measure contrast, color saturation, and brightness balance,
        then applies a focal-point calculation that looks at how visual weight is distributed
        across the frame. Those raw numbers are converted into a weighted score modeled on
        published research about visual attention, readability at small sizes, and how thumbnails
        are actually displayed across YouTube's mobile app, desktop homepage, search results, and
        suggested-video rail. We built it this way deliberately: no upload queue, no server storage
        of your images, and no waiting.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold text-ink dark:text-fog">Who VT Analyzer Is For</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink/65 dark:text-smoke sm:text-base">
        We built this tool for the full range of people who publish on YouTube — solo creators
        uploading their first video, established channels running weekly release schedules, video
        editors preparing deliverables for a client, social media managers juggling several
        channels at once, and small agencies that need a fast, consistent way to sanity-check a
        thumbnail before it goes live. Whether you're designing a MrBeast-style reaction thumbnail,
        a calm and minimal tutorial cover, a Fortnite or gaming highlight image, or a template
        exported from Canva, the same underlying readability and contrast principles apply, and the
        analyzer scores all of them on the same scale.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold text-ink dark:text-fog">Our Values</h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink/65 dark:text-smoke sm:text-base">
        <li><strong class="text-ink dark:text-fog">Privacy by default.</strong> Your thumbnail is analyzed on your device. We don't need a copy of it to help you improve it.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink dark:text-fog">Honest limitations.</strong> We'd rather tell you what a design tool can't promise than oversell a score.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink dark:text-fog">Free access first.</strong> The core analyzer stays free, because the creators who need feedback the most are often the ones just starting out.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink dark:text-fog">Plain-language explanations.</strong> Every score comes with a reason, not just a number, so you actually learn something you can reuse on the next thumbnail.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold text-ink dark:text-fog">Where We're Based</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink/65 dark:text-smoke sm:text-base">
        VT Analyzer is operated from our office at 202, Princes Business Park, Vijay Nagar, Indore,
        Madhya Pradesh, India. While the tool serves creators worldwide, we keep a real office and a
        small, hands-on team specifically so support requests get a human response rather than an
        automated one.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-ink/10 dark:border-seam bg-paper-dim dark:bg-night-dim py-16 text-center">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-xl px-5">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold text-ink dark:text-fog">Have a question or feature idea?</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-ink/60 dark:text-smoke">We read every message.</p>
      <a href="/contact/" class="mt-6 inline-block rounded-sm bg-red px-6 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-white hover:bg-red-dark">
        Get in Touch
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
